      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"balzac-illusions-perdues-61fbb32d86fa426a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Du Châtelet presents his love for Naïs as an enduring fact while promising to prove it through continuing service. This strongly supports T and independently supports P through the explicit future undertaking. Nothing indicates that the words escape as an affective or reflex-like discharge, so emotional devotion does not warrant E.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The extent of P depends on whether the core is restricted to the embedded clause « combien je vous aime » or includes its governing promise, « en toute occasion je vous prouverai ». The full utterance clearly supplies undertaking force; his sincerity remains uncertain but does not alter its T/P mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Du Châtelet speaks the love avowal directly to Naïs in private dialogue, syntactically embedding it within a promise that he will repeatedly prove how much he loves her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal forms part of an elaborate declaration of permanent devotion after Naïs has rejected his suit: he offers his life, promises faithful service, and positions himself as her defender. The narration also frames him as « heureux » when the scandal spreads and shows that his earlier defense worsened the gossip, creating substantial suspicion that the declaration is calculated courtship or opportunistic self-presentation. Such possible manipulation bears on sincerity, not on the utterance's strongly avowing and undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« mon cœur sera toujours à vous, et en toute occasion je vous prouverai combien je vous aime »\",\"supports\":\"The enduring-heart claim presents love as true (T), while the promise to prove it on every occasion independently supports an undertaking (P).\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Disposez d'une vie qui vous appartient » and « je veillerai sur vous comme un fidèle serviteur, sans espoir de récompense »\",\"supports\":\"The avowal belongs to an explicit pledge of continuing personal service, strengthening P rather than merely indicating emotional intensity.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Naïs has previously « repoussé [ses] vœux », and du Châtelet renews his devotion while offering to become her acknowledged defender.\",\"supports\":\"This is a deliberate renewal of courtship and allegiance, not evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the town is in uproar, « l'heureux du Châtelet » goes to Naïs; the narration says his defense had fanned the gossip.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding narration raises doubts about sincerity and suggests strategic opportunism, while leaving the T/P force of the spoken declaration intact.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified in Honoré de Balzac's French novel Illusions perdues as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its grammatical presentation as current love.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the avowal of an enduring state and the accompanying promise of continued proof and service. Strategic or manipulative context does not create a missing core-force dimension, and there is no independent evidence for E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The analytical translation was used only as an aid; quoted evidence is taken from the supplied French source text.\"}"
        }
